KosmicKrisp: argument tables borrowed per recording, not one per VkCommandBuffer

Every VkCommandBuffer owned an MTL4ArgumentTable from creation to
destruction; Metal keeps 96 KB of host memory behind each, whatever its
three buffer bindings. A game allocates command buffers by the thousand and
keeps them: Counter-Strike 2 had 3,530 alive after four minutes (330 MB of
the game's 925 MB of untyped malloc blocks; heap of the process, stage 61).
Metal snapshots a table's contents when a draw, dispatch or execute command
is encoded (MTL4RenderCommandEncoder.h, setArgumentTable:stages:), so a
table is only needed while its command buffer records: a command buffer now
borrows one of the device's idle tables at vkBeginCommandBuffer and returns
it at vkEndCommandBuffer, at a reset while recording, or when destroyed.
The pool holds as many tables as command buffers ever recorded at once.

--- a/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_cmd_buffer.c
+++ b/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_cmd_buffer.c
@@ -79,6 +79,58 @@
    assert(es->cmd_buf == NULL);
 
    util_dynarray_fini(&es->ts_resolves);
